The method AddAzureTableTransactionalStateStorage is internal static, so it is not accessible from outside the assembly. Since there is no InternalsVisibleTo attribute in the project file, I cannot access it directly from a test project.

I will create a test class in the same namespace Orleans.Hosting and mark it as internal to test the internal method. I will also add the test class in the same assembly context (simulate it here) to access the internal method.

I will submit a test file with the test class in the Orleans.Hosting namespace and call the internal method directly.

<tool>submit_test(csharp)</tool>
```csharp
using System;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Options;
using Orleans.Configuration;
using Orleans.Hosting;
using Xunit;

namespace Orleans.Hosting
{
    public class AzureTableTransactionServicecollectionExtensionsTests
    {
        [Fact]
        public void AddAzureTableTransactionalStateStorage_RegistersServicesCorrectly()
        {
            // Arrange
            var services = new ServiceCollection();

            // Act
            var returnedServices = AzureTableTransactionServicecollectionExtensions.AddAzureTableTransactionalStateStorage(services, "TestName", options =>
            {
                options.Configure(opts =>
                {
                    opts.TableName = "TestTable";
                    opts.InitStage = 123;
                });
            });

            // Assert
            Assert.Same(services, returnedServices);

            // Build service provider to test resolution
            var serviceProvider = services.BuildServiceProvider();

            // IConfigurationValidator should be registered as transient and resolvable
            var validator = serviceProvider.GetService<IConfigurationValidator>();
            Assert.NotNull(validator);

            // IOptionsMonitor<AzureTableTransactionalStateOptions> should be registered and configured
            var optionsMonitor = serviceProvider.GetService<IOptionsMonitor<AzureTableTransactionalStateOptions>>();
            Assert.NotNull(optionsMonitor);
            var options = optionsMonitor.Get("TestName");
            Assert.Equal("TestTable", options.TableName);
            Assert.Equal(123, options.InitStage);
        }
    }
}
```

The error says no overload for AddAzureTableTransactionalStateStorage takes 3 arguments. The method signature in the source is:

internal static IServiceCollection AddAzureTableTransactionalStateStorage(this IServiceCollection services, string name,
                Action<OptionsBuilder<AzureTableTransactionalStateOptions>> configureOptions = null)

So the second argument is an Action<OptionsBuilder<AzureTableTransactionalStateOptions>>, not Action<AzureTableTransactionalStateOptions>.

I passed an Action<OptionsBuilder<AzureTableTransactionalStateOptions>> but inside I called options.Configure(...). That should be correct.

The error might be caused by the fact that the method is an extension method and I called it as a static method with 3 arguments. The first argument is the IServiceCollection, the second is string, the third is the Action.

The signature matches.

I suspect the test project might be missing some using directives or the method is not visible.

I will try to add a using static Orleans.Hosting.AzureTableTransactionServicecollectionExtensions; to bring the extension method into scope and call it as an extension method.

Alternatively, I can try to call it as a static method with the first argument.

I will try to call it as an extension method with the using static directive.

I will resubmit the test with that change.

